[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Integer Overflows, Precision Loss, and Rounding Errors in Web3"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Smart contracts handle vast amounts of financial value. Unlike traditional software where an arithmetic error might crash a server, in Web3, it can lead to catastrophic loss of funds. This tutorial explores the three most common arithmetic pitfalls in smart contract development: Integer Overflows/Underflows, Precision Loss, and Rounding Errors."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Integer Overflows and Underflows"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An integer overflow occurs when an arithmetic operation attempts to create a numeric value that is outside of the range that can be represented by a given number of bits. In Solidity versions prior to 0.8.0, developers had to use external libraries like SafeMath to prevent these issues."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract OverflowExample {
    uint8 public maxLimit = 255;

    // In Solidity 0.8+, this automatically reverts with a panic error (0x11)
    function addOne(uint8 _val) public pure returns (uint8) {
        return _val + 1;
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Overflows wrap around to zero (e.g., 255 + 1 = 0 in uint8)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Underflows wrap around to the maximum value (e.g., 0 - 1 = 255 in uint8)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use Solidity >=0.8.0 or explicitly use checked arithmetic to avoid unexpected state mutations."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Precision Loss in Division"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"EVM (Ethereum Virtual Machine) does not support floating-point arithmetic. All numbers are integers. When performing division before multiplication, precision loss frequently occurs due to truncation."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"// Bad: Division happens first, losing the fractional part
uint256 badResult = (5 / 10) * 1e18; // Results in 0

// Good: Multiplication happens first, preserving precision
uint256 goodResult = (5 * 1e18) / 10; // Results in 500000000000000000"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Rounding Errors in DeFi Protocols"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Rounding errors happen when calculating shares, fees, or interest. Attackers can exploit rounding errors through "inflation attacks" or by gaming liquidity pools where small remainders favor the protocol or the user unjustly over hundreds of transactions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always scale up your calculations using fixed-point math representations (like 1e18 decimals)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be cautious when calculating user shares in staking or vault contracts; round in favor of the contract."},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding these fundamental arithmetic vulnerabilities, developers can write safer, more robust Web3 applications and protect user assets from exploitation."}]
